character handling logic... Still writing the handler... Still writing flag parsing... I'm realizing I need to consider long flags like `--number` and `--number-nonblank`, but the PRD and test cases only specify short flags for cat, so I'll stick with just handling those and report any unrecognized options as errors. Handling unrecognized long flags...\n\nSince the test suite only uses short flags and the PRD doesn't require long flag support, I'll reject any unrecognized long options while focusing on the short flags that are actually specified.\n\nLet me trace through the acceptance criteria to verify my implementation handles the core requirements correctly. Now I'm checking the `-b` flag behavior with multiple consecutive blank lines—it should number only non-blank lines while preserving the blank lines themselves. Then I'm starting to verify the `-s` flag, which squeezes multiple blank lines into a single blank line.\n\nFor the `-v` flag test, I'm working through how control characters and special bytes should be displayed: control characters get converted to their caret notation (like `^A` for 0x01), tabs remain unchanged, DEL becomes `^?`, and high bytes need their own handling.\n\nNow I'm checking the `-A` flag test case, which combines `-v`, `-E`, and `-T` together, so I need to trace through how a control character at the start of a line gets processed.\n\nLooking at the remaining test cases: when a file doesn't exist, the error goes to stderr and the exit code becomes 1, but processing continues with the next file. And for edge cases like empty input, the line counter stays at 1 and nothing gets written, which is the expected behavior.\n\nI'm also considering potential issues with multiple stdin arguments—each `-` would read from the stream sequentially, matching GNU cat's behavior. Write errors during transformation are caught in the processTransformed function, and io.Copy handles them in the direct copy path. But I'm noticing something in the main loop